# Managed by Ansible (tailscale role) - do not edit by hand.
# Boot ordering (epic 21 #04): start Docker after the Tailscale daemon so the caddy-relay, which binds
# this VPS's Tailscale address, usually finds it on the first try.
#
# ORDERING ONLY — deliberately no Requires=/Wants=/BindsTo=/PartOf=. A Tailscale outage (daemon stopped,
# failed, or the tailnet unreachable) must never be able to keep Docker, and with it the whole public
# stack, from starting. After= only sequences the two when both are being started in the same boot: if
# tailscaled is stopped or fails, Docker starts anyway; if it is merely slow, Docker waits for its start
# job to finish (bounded by systemd's start timeout). The caddy-relay's restart policy covers the case
# where the daemon is up but tailscale0 has no address yet (docs/reboot-resilience.md).
[Unit]
After=tailscaled.service
